#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""orionx-logquery — forensic query and local threat correlation for Orion-X.

@decision DEC-PHASE12-027
@title logquery ported into Orion-X as a multi-source, intel-aged IR tool
@status accepted
@rationale Ported, with the owner's authorization, from jarocki-edge
  scripts/logquery.py. The upstream tool's real value is not its SQL: it is
  the judgement it makes from the operator's own history — did this source
  come back on other days, did it also load real pages, is the thing it probed
  something almost nothing else here has ever asked for. scanwatch
  (DEC-PHASE12-022) can tell the Cockpit that one host touched 900 ports; it
  cannot tell the operator whether that host is singling this deck out. This
  tool can, and that gap is why it was worth porting.

  Three things changed in the port, each with its own annotation in the
  module that owns it:

  - Sources (logquery_sources.py). Upstream fetches from R2 and re-ingests its
    own NDJSON. Here local evidence is the default and needs no credentials,
    with r2, s3 and api as equal peers.
  - Intel (logquery_intel.py). Upstream reads a build-time file and never
    mentions its age. Here the cache carries a recorded fetch time and every
    run states it; a stale cache says so in the banner, in JSON output and on
    the R.A.I.N. bus.
  - Engine (logquery_engine.py). Upstream hard-requires DuckDB. Here stdlib
    SQLite is the default and DuckDB is an opt-in accelerator, so a report
    runs on a stock boot.

@decision DEC-PHASE12-027
@title Findings are published to the R.A.I.N. bus, not just printed
@status accepted
@rationale A forensic conclusion that exists only in a terminal an operator
  has already closed is not part of the deck's awareness. Targeted verdicts
  and a stale-intel warning are emitted via the orionx-event CLI, the same
  fire-and-forget Popen pattern as orionx-nucleotide-watch and scanwatch, so
  the event stream, the threat-pressure gauge and the audible cue all respond
  without a second path into the UI.

SECURITY MODEL (preserved from upstream)
  - Credentials are read at runtime from an operator-owned file, mode-checked,
    and never written to the repo, the bus, or any output.
  - Sealed records are decrypted with an offline private key. No code path here
    weakens, caches, or transmits key material; a decrypt failure is counted,
    never echoed, because the exception text can carry ciphertext fragments.
  - Decrypted plaintext stays in memory unless --keep is passed, and --keep
    writes 0600 and says so.
"""
